    "body": "<p>A tropical rainforest at dawn is the richest terrestrial soundscape on earth. Howler monkeys roar from the canopy, toucans and parrots call in loud bursts, tinamous whistle from the forest floor, frogs finish their night choruses, and insects, especially cicadas, rise as the sun warms the canopy. Rain adds a sudden, drowning geophony when a shower passes, and the chorus rebuilds within minutes after it stops. The acoustic niche organisation is most visible here: dozens of species sing at once, yet each occupies its own band and time pattern, so a trained listener can pick out individual voices from the wall of sound. Rainforest soundscapes are also the most threatened by logging and fragmentation, and long-term recordings from the same forest plots document the thinning of the dawn chorus as species disappear.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: rainforest soundscape recordings analysed by band show the densest spectral occupancy of any terrestrial habitat, with species partitioned by frequency and time as the acoustic niche hypothesis predicts. Long-term recording programmes in tropical forests document declines in acoustic richness following logging and fragmentation, and playback and survey studies confirm that many rainforest species are detectable by sound long before they are seen.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if a rainforest dawn recording holds 40 vocal species and each occupies an average band of 400 hertz, the total occupied bandwidth is 40 x 400 = 16000 hertz, which exceeds the 10000-hertz audible band used in this example, so overlap is unavoidable and time-sharing must help. The demo prints total occupied 16000 Hz, band width 10000 Hz, and the excess, 16000 - 10000 = 6000 Hz.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published tropical soundscape ecology literature (rainforest dawn chorus and fragmentation studies); figures as published October 2026</p>",
